<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>Keep Contact</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>armv7</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Native auth deep link. Mirrors the Android manifest's
	     com.keepcontact.app://auth intent filter; without it the Supabase
	     callback cannot return into the app and sign-in never completes. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.keepcontact.app</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.keepcontact.app</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!-- Passive liveness evidence (KC-IOS-UNLOCK-SPIKE-001, ADR-0039).
	     remote-notification is the primary path: the server wakes the app with a
	     silent push and the app answers with the current lock state. A silent
	     push is best-effort — iOS may delay it, coalesce it, or not deliver it
	     at all, so it may not wake the app and cannot on its own prove that
	     anyone is being watched. There is no continuous location session; that
	     is what previously put an indicator in the status bar and drew a
	     battery baseline.
	     location is declared only because significant-change monitoring is the
	     one documented way iOS may relaunch an app after a force-quit. It is
	     best-effort and frequently does not happen; no coordinate is ever read,
	     stored, or transmitted, and it is never treated as evidence that the
	     person is fine. Whether to keep this capability at all before App
	     Review is a Store decision for the human, recorded rather than assumed. -->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
		<string>location</string>
	</array>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact may use this to relaunch itself in the background if the app is closed. It is best-effort and often does not happen, so it is not a guarantee that anyone is watching over you. Keep Contact never reads, stores, or sends your location for this, and it does not track your movements.</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact may use this to relaunch itself in the background if the app is closed. It is best-effort and often does not happen, so it is not a guarantee that anyone is watching over you. Keep Contact never reads, stores, or sends your location for this, and it does not track your movements.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact 需要使用摄像头扫描二维码，以加入守护圈或同步登录凭据。</string>
	<!-- HealthKit is used as a wake source, not as a data source
	     (KC-IOS-HEALTHWAKE-SPIKE-001). KC observes that new step data exists so
	     iOS will relaunch it while it is closed; it never sends step counts or
	     any other health data anywhere. Read-only, so no NSHealthUpdate string. -->
	<!-- CoreMotion: step history, activity type and the brief accelerometer
	     sample that tells a phone in a hand from one lying on a table. Missing
	     this string is a hard crash the moment CMPedometer is touched, not a
	     silent refusal. -->
	<key>NSMotionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact reads your phone's own step and movement history so your circle can tell you are fine without you opening the app. It never records where you go, and no movement data leaves your phone beyond a count.</string>
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact checks that your phone has recorded recent movement, so your circle can tell you are fine without you having to open the app. Only the fact that you were active and the time is used — no health data ever leaves your phone.</string>
	<!-- KC requests `toShare: []` and never writes health data. This string is
	     here because App Store upload validation keys off the *entitlement*, not
	     off which APIs are actually called: a build carrying the HealthKit
	     entitlement is rejected without both strings even when the write half is
	     never used. Omitting it cost a whole TestFlight build (run 30833103571,
	     altool error 90683). The wording says plainly that nothing is written,
	     rather than inventing a purpose that does not exist. -->
	<key>NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact never writes anything to Health. It only reads whether your phone recorded recent movement, so your circle can tell you are fine without you having to open the app.</string>
	<!-- No custom cryptography; keeps TestFlight builds from stalling on the
	     export-compliance question at every upload. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
